# Track 4 — E2E round 2: 20 vCPU total budget
#
# Rebalanced after docker stats showed the token-issuer (RS256 signing) pinned
# at its cap while validators sat at ~5%. Budget moved validators -> issuer,
# with the request-handler kept matched so it isn't the next bottleneck.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | Redis x 3            | 0.15 x 3        | 0.45     |
# | request-handler      | 0.75 x 2        | 1.5      |
# | verifier-selector    | 0.4 x 1         | 0.4      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.95 x 2        | 1.9      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 15        | 15.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.15 x 5        | 0.75     |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 20.0     |
#
# Note: at this budget the token pipeline (issuer+handler) is intrinsically the
# ceiling (~400-450 jobs/s) — 15 verifiers could do more, but 5 vCPU of overhead
# can't sign/serve faster. This is a real service-model property, not a misconfig.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round2.env; set +a
REDIS_CPUS=0.15
HANDLER_REPLICAS=2
HANDLER_CPUS=0.75
SELECTOR_CPUS=0.4
ISSUER_REPLICAS=2
ISSUER_CPUS=0.95
SNARK_REPLICAS=15
SNARK_COUNT=15
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.15
